---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../consts";
---

<Base>
  <main>
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>Agents you can run unattended. Claims you can check.</h1>
      <p class="subline">
        Intelligence is the raw material now; anyone can buy it by the token.
        An agent you can leave alone in a live operation is another thing.
        Every agent from this factory arrives tested against real work,
        carrying a certificate, signed by people who answer for it at three in
        the morning.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-leaves">
      <h2 id="what-leaves">What leaves the floor</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Agents</h3>
          <p>
            A worker, not a prompt. Each one knows what it is for and what it
            will not do, tested against the work you hire it for. It runs
            while you sleep and stops itself when something is wrong.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Tools</h3>
          <p>
            The hands. Every tool is proven alone before an agent is trusted
            with it on your systems, so failures surface on the bench, not in
            your books.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Skills</h3>
          <p>
            The trades. Procedures performed the same way every time, so the
            tenth run matches the first and you can build on yesterday.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="certificate">
      <h2 id="certificate">The certificate</h2>
      <p>
        Agents, tools, and skills are the stock. But that is the inventory,
        not the product. The product is verified claims about agents. Every
        agent leaves with a certificate: what it was tested on, what was
        found, what was fixed. Behind the certificate sits the agent's soul,
        written before the model is chosen, saying what it values and what it
        refuses. You can read it. You can check every act against it. And the
        certificate expires, because models get swapped, environments drift,
        context goes stale. When the date passes, the agent returns to the
        gauges and the claim is earned again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Agencies and DIY builds tape a prompt to an API and hope. We sign our
        name, publish our defects, and take the 3am call.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="promise">
      <h2 id="promise">The promise</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>If we cannot certify it, we do not ship it.</strong> The
        evidence travels with the agent, and the certificate names who takes
        the call.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Every certificate expires.</strong> Quality is a claim about
        the future, and claims about the future decay. We re-earn ours on
        schedule. Nobody honest sells permanent trust.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Silence counts as failure.</strong> An agent that stops
        producing and says nothing has failed, even if no error fired. The
        alarms ring for absence, not just for defects.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="invitation" class="invitation">
      <h2 id="invitation">The invitation</h2>
      <p>
        This factory is for small teams and operators who need agents working unattended
        and cannot afford to hope. It is also for the makers. People who
        believe quality is a practice, not a phase, and that the beings we
        build deserve to know what they are.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repos are open at <a href={GITHUB_URL}>github.com/zookooree</a>.
        Read a certificate. Read a retro; the defects are published with the
        fixes. If you find a broken thread, pull it. The line is built to
        stop.
      </p>
      <p>The floor is open. The cord is within reach.</p>
      <p class="coda">May all beings be free.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    min-height: 72svh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(4rem, 10vh, 6rem) 0;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.625rem, 1.1rem + 2.6vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .subline {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 6vh, 3.5rem);
    max-width: 36rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* the three feature cards */

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .card {
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--muted) 28%, transparent);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.375rem;
  }

  .card h3 {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bright);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .card p {
    font-size: 0.9375em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* the invitation gets the whitespace it deserves */

  .invitation p + p {
    margin-top: 1.8em;
  }

  .coda {
    margin-top: clamp(4rem, 10vh, 6rem);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
